= The Lighthouse Garden

Name: #line(length: 60%) Date: #line(length: 30%)

Maya had lived in the small fishing town of Pebble Cove for only three weeks when she noticed the old lighthouse on the hill. Its white paint was peeling, and its windows were cracked, but a tiny garden grew around its base. Tomatoes climbed a wooden fence, and purple flowers swayed in the wind.

"Who takes care of that garden?" she asked her grandfather one morning.

"Mr. Okafor used to," he said. "He kept the lighthouse lamp lit every night for forty years. Since he moved to the city, nobody has tended the plants."

Maya thought about this all day at school. At lunch, she sketched a plan in her notebook: water the tomatoes, pull the weeds, and maybe paint a new sign for the gate.

After school, she borrowed a watering can from her grandfather's shed and climbed the hill. The garden was dry and tangled. She worked until the sun turned orange, pulling weeds until her fingers were sore.

The next week, something surprising happened. A small boy named Eli appeared at the fence with a bucket. "My grandmother says this was Mr. Okafor's garden," he said. "Can I help?"

Together they weeded, watered, and tied the tomato vines to the fence with strips of old cloth. Soon other townspeople began to stop by with seeds, tools, and fresh paint.

By autumn, the garden was bursting with color. Mr. Okafor visited on a crisp Saturday, leaning on his cane. He stood at the gate for a long time, then smiled. "You have kept my lighthouse alive," he said. "Thank you."

Maya realized that some of the best things in a town are not built by one person. They grow when neighbors decide to help.

